{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1832 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c6f5e32c2ce11a8fa5fa1abaaa919688188c6401c590baa7ed5f694b5b5752ee" :edb/db-before-t 1831 :edb/db-after-t 1832 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535144 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:57:56.639Z" 13194139535144 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333670 13194139535144 true] [17592186096545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333691 13194139535144 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333677 13194139535144 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333684 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333671 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1089 "q-114419" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1113 #uuid "2e1a1dd3-3388-5f3d-8d7a-a3ff07dd42a8" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333670 1145 "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points." 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333672 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333673 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333674 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333675 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333676 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1041 "selection" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333676 13194139535144 true] [17592186333671 1110 #uuid "f4caa3e0-51b9-5fe0-9280-4fa651b69b35" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333672 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=2$" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333672 1111 #uuid "addff524-8fb9-5760-a710-1eaf0806f2cc" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333673 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333673 1111 #uuid "bc6936e8-d65a-51c6-a47e-c9913e49b7f2" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333674 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333674 1111 #uuid "c6638664-b046-596c-9880-f021d212a6f5" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333675 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333675 1111 #uuid "8ab2dd6b-9c79-5fe4-9925-637166131c3a" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333676 1034 "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333676 1111 #uuid "645c3376-2379-5b97-9d16-5b2e31efb73b" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333678 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1089 "q-21044" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1113 #uuid "f01f8756-8970-5015-bf48-1f08a36f1f26" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333677 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333679 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333680 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333681 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333682 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333683 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1041 "selection" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333679 13194139535144 true] [17592186333678 1110 #uuid "5e99b56f-26de-5c42-a6c2-058da69e7299" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333679 1034 "x=-1" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333679 1111 #uuid "bf1f1ddb-0270-5e1d-adff-a3b8d9343b08" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333680 1034 "x=1" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333680 1111 #uuid "3e5656ac-ac8f-5055-b399-4151121efff7" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333681 1034 "x=-3" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333681 1111 #uuid "e8041d77-d7eb-58cc-8ea9-6063c5e44c3e" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333682 1034 "x=6" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333682 1111 #uuid "414ea75b-8727-5abf-83c0-7d6fa0792fcc" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333683 1034 "x=0" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333683 1111 #uuid "53d9943a-8225-5215-ae4f-3a22c803bbb9" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333685 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1089 "q-21045" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1113 #uuid "17c2a431-b9a6-5e59-b365-e7f1bd9e8f80" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333684 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333686 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333687 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333688 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333689 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333690 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1041 "selection" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333686 13194139535144 true] [17592186333685 1110 #uuid "25f144ac-f989-5ca1-a069-599509696ca2" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333686 1034 "x=1" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333686 1111 #uuid "d249f77e-2330-5878-bf0f-eed7a9569b47" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333687 1034 "x=3" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333687 1111 #uuid "0a48152b-7428-5af0-b317-2a40ba1fca1f" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333688 1034 "x=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333688 1111 #uuid "a7789eb0-c545-500f-9db4-c62c1aae5fc6" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333689 1034 "x=2" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333689 1111 #uuid "d400d042-6b8d-5919-88e8-7968b6797b48" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333690 1034 "x=\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333690 1111 #uuid "d1ef19e4-9c76-5d48-af86-c3384933995e" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333692 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1089 "q-79203" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/2c0a892cd217758e2871f2d3f6f18d7615e79e0f5ad792c751c662e35c69e168.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1113 #uuid "c7d0fd56-6605-5190-9333-e6bf3c07cfdf" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333691 1145 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333693 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333694 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333695 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333696 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333697 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1041 "selection" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333695 13194139535144 true] [17592186333692 1110 #uuid "a3ae0959-70ec-5fca-85ba-b0492538a831" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333693 1034 "x=2" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333693 1111 #uuid "958f54a6-95cb-5192-8a3a-e12c13b3e6e3" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333694 1034 "x=0" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535144 true] [17592186333694 1111 #uuid "6ae5b1bb-5e50-5eb2-9662-afa9cb07a12b" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333695 1034 "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535144 true] [17592186333695 1111 #uuid "f73b1650-e5a9-54ac-bfeb-d76db311d3c8" 13194139535144 true] [17592186333696 1034 "